{"records":[{"id":"3sdsc83vsn","title":"Genome-Scale Exon Perturbation Screens Uncover Exons Critical for Cell Fitness","description":"CRISPR-Cas technology has transformed functional genomics, yet understanding how individual exons differentially shape cellular phenotypes remains limited. Here, we optimized and conducted massively parallel exon deletion and splice site mutation screens in human cell lines to identify exons that regulate cellular fitness. Fitness-promoting exons are prevalent in essential and highly expressed genes, and commonly overlap with protein domains and interaction interfaces. Conversely, fitness-suppressing exons are enriched in non-essential genes, exhibiting lower inclusion levels, and overlap with intrinsically disordered regions and disease-associated mutations. In-depth mechanistic investigation of the TAF5 alternative exon-8, screen hit, revealed that its inclusion is required for assembly of the TFIID general transcription initiation complex, thereby regulating global gene expression output. Collectively, our orthogonal exon perturbation screens established a comprehensive repository of phenotypically important exons and uncovered regulatory mechanisms governing cellular fitness and gene expression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-20T08:55:47.576Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3sdsc83vsn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3sdsc83vsn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mei-sheng","last_name":"Xiao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arun Prasath","last_name":"Damodaran","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bandana","last_name":"Kumari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ethan","last_name":"Dickson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kun","last_name":"Xing","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tyler","last_name":"On","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nikhil","last_name":"Parab","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Helen E. ","last_name":"King","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexendar R.","last_name":"Perez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wilfried M. ","last_name":"Guiblet","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gerard","last_name":"Duncan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anney","last_name":"Che","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Raj","last_name":"Chari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thorkell","last_name":"Andresson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joana A.","last_name":"Vidigal","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert J.","last_name":"Weatheritt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Aregger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Gonatopoulos-Pournatzis","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bpcpfns6vb","title":"Global genetic interaction network of a human cell maps conserved principles and informs functional interpretation of gene co-essentiality profiles. Billmann et al.","description":"Deciphering how genes interact within human cells is essential for understanding their functional wiring and the development of targeted therapeutic strategies. In this study, we present a genome-scale map of genetic interactions in the human haploid cell line HAP1, based on CRISPR-based perturbation of ~ 4 million gene pairs. The resulting network comprises ~89,000 high-confidence gene-gene interactions, organizing genes into hierarchical modules corresponding to protein complexes and pathways, biological processes, and cellular compartments, mirroring principles observed in yeast and highlighting the functional architecture of a human cell. This large-scale genetic network complements the DepMap gene co-essentiality network by capturing unique functional information, uncovering roles of previously uncharacterized genes, and identifying molecular determinants of cancer cell line-specific genetic dependencies. This study presents a general data-driven strategy analysis for systematically exploring the roles of genes and their functional connections in human cell lines.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-18T10:15:12.084Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bpcpfns6vb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bpcpfns6vb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maximilian","last_name":"Billmann","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Costanzo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiang","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arshia Z.","last_name":"Hassan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mahfuzur","last_name":"Rahman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kevin R.","last_name":"Brown","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katherine S.","last_name":"Chan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amy Hin Yan","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carles","last_name":"Pons","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Henry N.","last_name":"Ward","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Catherine","last_name":"Ross","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jolanda","last_name":"van Leeuwen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Aregger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Keith A.","last_name":"Lawson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amy F.","last_name":"Roth","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nesli E.","last_name":"Sen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Duncan T.","last_name":"Forster","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guihong","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Patricia","last_name":"Mero","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna N.","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yoonkyu","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Magali","last_name":"Aguilera-Uribe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matej","last_name":"Usaj","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sylvia M. T.","last_name":"Almeida","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kamaldeep","last_name":"Aulakh","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Urvi","last_name":"Bhojoo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Saba","last_name":"Birkadze","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nathaniel","last_name":"Budijono","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xunhui",